WEBVTT

00:00.330 --> 00:04.182
Martin volunteers you to organise a family gathering, although you

00:04.195 --> 00:05.878
agreed only to bring food.

00:06.288 --> 00:11.084
When you object, he says you remembered it wrong and calls you unreliable to others.

00:11.492 --> 00:13.112
You wonder what to call this.

00:13.507 --> 00:15.892
Start with the agreement and what he did.

00:16.300 --> 00:20.624
You can correct the claim and decide what work you accept without knowing every

00:20.637 --> 00:22.257
reason behind his behaviour.

00:23.288 --> 00:27.969
The conversations in this example assume it is reasonably safe to disagree.

00:28.379 --> 00:34.144
If there are threats, fear, monitoring, or control over housing, money or care, start

00:34.157 --> 00:36.210
with support and a safer next step.

00:36.593 --> 00:40.840
Abuse is not simply a communication problem both people must solve.

00:41.249 --> 00:46.044
You do not need to confront someone or establish a diagnosis before seeking help.

00:47.080 --> 00:51.991
In this book, manipulation means influencing someone in a way that makes it harder to

00:52.003 --> 00:54.248
choose freely with the information they need.

00:54.658 --> 00:58.790
If Martin knowingly tells others you agreed to organise, he may use their

00:58.803 --> 01:00.499
expectations to pressure you.

01:00.882 --> 01:03.165
That describes a particular action.

01:03.573 --> 01:07.451
It does not diagnose his personality or show that every disputed

01:07.464 --> 01:09.122
agreement is manipulation.

01:10.163 --> 01:14.819
Gaslighting is psychological abuse that undermines someone’s trust in their memory,

01:15.049 --> 01:16.783
perceptions or judgment.

01:17.192 --> 01:20.879
One disagreement about memory is not enough to establish it.

01:21.261 --> 01:26.159
Paige Sweet’s research explains how unequal power and other people’s authority can

01:26.172 --> 01:28.710
help make a victim’s account seem untrustworthy.

01:29.118 --> 01:32.460
It does not provide a checklist for diagnosing a person.

01:32.856 --> 01:37.320
Repeated denial, fear or attempts to discredit you can warrant support

01:37.333 --> 01:38.736
without settling the label.

01:39.747 --> 01:43.701
A message saying you can bring food may help clarify the agreement.

01:44.110 --> 01:48.510
If safe and useful, keep that record or a brief note, separating

01:48.523 --> 01:50.028
recorded words from memory.

01:50.397 --> 01:53.816
But a monitored device or a threat changes the decision.

01:54.186 --> 01:57.374
Collecting or storing evidence could increase risk.

01:57.783 --> 02:02.171
You do not need perfect records before getting help, and you do not have to make the

02:02.184 --> 02:03.766
person admit what happened.

02:04.788 --> 02:08.360
A request asks Martin to check before offering your time.

02:08.743 --> 02:13.246
An agreement states that you bring food and someone else organises the venue.

02:13.654 --> 02:18.488
A boundary is a limit backed by an action you can take: I will not take on tasks

02:18.501 --> 02:20.210
assigned without my agreement.

02:20.579 --> 02:24.368
Choose something you can act on, rather than a threat you cannot or do

02:24.380 --> 02:25.885
not intend to carry out.

02:26.913 --> 02:31.071
You might say: I agreed to bring food, not organise the gathering.

02:31.481 --> 02:33.024
I can still bring food.

02:33.393 --> 02:36.302
Please correct the message saying I am organising.

02:36.710 --> 02:40.448
If pressure continues, you can repeat the limit without arguing

02:40.460 --> 02:42.080
endlessly about your character.

02:42.450 --> 02:46.072
Dependence on someone may limit what is safe or possible.

02:46.481 --> 02:50.919
Difficulty enforcing a limit does not mean you are weak or consent to harm.

02:51.955 --> 02:56.891
An apology can be followed by correcting the message, finding another organiser and

02:56.904 --> 02:59.098
asking before offering your time again.

02:59.507 --> 03:01.548
Those changes address the problem.

03:01.956 --> 03:06.191
If it repeats, fewer commitments or outside advice may be needed.

03:06.599 --> 03:11.038
You can correct your own unclear wording without taking responsibility for someone

03:11.050 --> 03:12.964
else’s coercion or abuse.

03:13.333 --> 03:16.203
Check whether you also accept other people’s no.

03:17.247 --> 03:21.035
When pressured to accept something harmful or unwanted, ask:

03:21.367 --> 03:22.961
What can I safely do next?

03:23.370 --> 03:27.248
In an ordinary safe disagreement, describe the conduct and choose

03:27.260 --> 03:28.536
a limit you can act on.

03:28.944 --> 03:32.681
Where there is fear or control, choose a support step instead.

03:33.051 --> 03:36.163
Do not keep notes if discovery could put you at risk.

03:36.559 --> 03:40.513
Understanding should help you respond, not keep you in harm’s way.
